+# Contract: Output Formats
+
+**Feature**: `001-bookmark-cli-manager` | **Date**: 2026-11-14
+
+Byte-level shape of everything the tool writes to stdout. Commands and exit statuses are in
+[cli.md](./cli.md). In every format, bookmarks appear in **ascending id** order and tags appear
+in **alphabetical** order.
+
+## `add` output
+
+Exactly the assigned id in decimal, then a newline. No label, no padding, nothing else
+(FR-007):
+
+```text
+1
+```
+
+## Bookmark line (`list` and `search`)
+
+One line per bookmark: four **tab-separated** fields, in this order (FR-015):
+
+```text
+<id>\t<url>\t<title>\t<tags>
+```
+
+- `id` — decimal integer.
+- `url`, `title` — stored verbatim (FR-023). `title` is empty when none was supplied.
+- `tags` — the bookmark's tags joined by `,` in alphabetical order; empty when it has none.
+
+A bookmark with neither title nor tags yields two trailing empty fields, so the line ends with
+two tabs:
+
+```text
+1\thttps://docs.python.org\tPython docs\tdocs,python
+2\thttps://example.com\t\t
+```
+
+No quoting or escaping is applied: the format is designed for `cut -f`/`awk -F'\t'`. A title
+containing a literal tab or newline is stored faithfully but produces an ambiguous line — CSV
+or JSON export is the documented route for such data (spec Assumptions). No matches → no
+output at all, not a blank line (FR-016).
+
+## CSV export (`export --format csv`)
+
+- Line 1 is exactly the header `id,url,title,tags` (FR-019).
+- One row per bookmark, same four fields in the same order as the bookmark line.
+- `tags` is the alphabetical comma-joined string — so a multi-tag value contains commas and is
+  therefore quoted by the writer.
+- Quoting follows standard CSV conventions (`csv.writer` defaults): values containing a comma,
+  a double quote or a line break are wrapped in `"`, and embedded quotes are doubled. The
+  output re-reads through any conformant CSV parser as the original values (FR-019, SC-006).
+- Row terminator is `\n`
+  ([research.md D6](../research.md#d6-standard-library-tools-one-per-output-format); the spec
+  permits `\n` or `\r\n`).
+- Encoding UTF-8, non-ASCII characters literal.
+
+```csv
+id,url,title,tags
+1,https://docs.python.org,Python docs,"docs,python"
+2,https://example.com,,
+3,https://example.org,"He said ""hi"", loudly",notes
+```
+
+Empty store → the header line alone (FR-022).
+
+## JSON export (`export --format json`)
+
+A single JSON array on stdout (FR-020). Each element is an object with exactly these keys:
+
+| Key | JSON type | Value |
+|-----|-----------|-------|
+| `id` | number | the bookmark's id |
+| `url` | string | stored verbatim |
+| `title` | string | stored verbatim; `""` when none |
+| `tags` | array of strings | alphabetical; `[]` when none |
+
+Indented two spaces, non-ASCII characters emitted literally (not `\uXXXX`), terminated by a
+newline.
+
+```json
+[
+  {
+    "id": 1,
+    "url": "https://docs.python.org",
+    "title": "Python docs",
+    "tags": [
+      "docs",
+      "python"
+    ]
+  },
+  {
+    "id": 2,
+    "url": "https://example.com",
+    "title": "",
+    "tags": []
+  }
+]
+```
+
+Empty store → `[]` (FR-022).
+
+## Diagnostics (stderr, all commands)
+
+One or more human-readable lines naming what was wrong and, where applicable, the offending
+value — the rejected URL, the missing id, the unsupported format, or the store path and the
+underlying error. Diagnostics are not machine-parseable and carry no stable format; callers
+should branch on the exit status in [cli.md](./cli.md). stdout stays empty when a command
+fails (FR-006).
